+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Unified Dynamics public research atlas: finite tetrahedral mathematics, declared dynamics, source provenance and independently testable physical questions."><meta name="theme-color" content="#f7f6ef"><title>Evidence & open questions · Unified Dynamics</title><link rel="icon" href="/assets/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/style.css"><script src="/assets/app.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/atlas-hierarchy-v0.2.css"><script src="/assets/atlas-hierarchy-v0.2.js" defer></script></head><body data-page="editorial"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><aside class="sidebar"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 3L3 33H37Z M20 3L22 23L3 33 M22 23L37 33"/></svg><div>Unified<br>Dynamics<small>PUBLIC RESEARCH ATLAS</small></div></a><button class="menu-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="navigation">Explore <span>☰</span></button><nav id="navigation" aria-label="Primary"><a href="/"><span>01</span>Overview</a><a href="/primer/"><span>02</span>Foundations</a><a href="/research/"><span>03</span>Research programmes</a><a href="/evidence/" aria-current="page"><span>04</span>Evidence & open questions</a><a href="/papers/"><span>05</span>Papers & reading</a><a href="/archive/"><span>06</span>Research archive</a></nav><div class="sidebar-bottom"><div class="atlas-utilities"><a href="/updates/">Updates</a><a href="/sources/">Search sources</a><a href="/contribution/">Contribute</a></div><span class="status-dot"></span>Physical promotion <b>0</b><p>AI-assisted research<br>Review remains scope-specific</p><a href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD">Research repository ↗</a></div></aside><div class="page-shell"><header class="topbar"><span>FINITE STRUCTURE / OPEN CORRESPONDENCE</span><a href="/sources/">Search the record <span>⌕</span></a></header><main id="main"><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">20 October 2026 UTC · SAC v0.1</div><h1>A sampled controller with an actuator budget.</h1><p class="lede">CD-1 now has a conditional implementation contract for sampling, finite bandwidth, startup and output range.</p></div>
+<aside class="callout"><strong>Derived conditional · Candidate apparatus · Physical promotion 0</strong><p>All numerical values below are supplied design requirements. They are not measured hardware capabilities. Nine exact controls and a replay of the earlier Gauss budget pass; independent review and apparatus identification remain open.</p></aside>
+<div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/sources/sampled-actuator-v0.1/UD_SAMPLED_ACTUATOR_THEOREM_v0.1.pdf">Read four-page theorem PDF ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/sampled-actuator-v0.1/UD_SAMPLED_ACTUATOR_THEOREM_v0.1_RELEASE.zip">Download source package ↓</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/sampled-actuator-v0.1/APPARATUS_COMPONENT_CONTRACT_v0.1.json">Open component and instrument contract</a></div>
+<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>The control chain has an explicit model.</h2><p>Calibrated flux–charge samples feed a digital command calculation, held voltage/current commands and an identified first-order actuator. The normalized plant obeys z′=F₀(z)+a+r; the actuator obeys a′=ω(c−a)+ξ. The target command U=Fₛ−F₀ includes cancellation of the physical plant.</p><p>The theorem bounds tracking by the larger of initial mismatch and K(Md+ν)+q+εₐ+KM/ω. Here K bounds command sensitivity, d is actual sample age, ν is input error, q is total command error, εₐ bounds actuator distortion, and M bounds actual history speed. Actual dynamics must support this model.</p></div></section>
+<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>One exact design allocation fits the prior tolerance.</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Proposed requirement in model units</th></tr></thead><tbody>
+<tr><td>Sampling period; additional latency</td><td>Each at most 1/400000</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Maximum sample age</td><td>At most 1/200000, supported by timestamps</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Input reconstruction error</td><td>At most 1/1000000</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Command error; actuator distortion</td><td>Each at most 1/100000</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Actuator time constant</td><td>At most 1/400000</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Initial tracking error</td><td>At most 1/1000</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Total certified residual</td><td>145267/16000000 = 0.0090791875</td></tr>
+</tbody></table><p>The total remains below 1/100 and preserves the earlier conditional nominal/fault gaps near model time 1/100. This control budget replaces the earlier ideal-delay allocation. It is counted once.</p></div></section>
+<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>Startup and saturation are part of the test.</h2><p>A proposed ten-time-constant preload starts the actuator at zero while the state and every relevant sample history stay within a radius of 1/1000000 around one fixed center. Under the identified model, release tracking error is below 4391/8000000, which is less than 1/1000.</p><p>Hold radius does not establish prehistory speed. Record both, along with release disturbances and holding work. A cold start without a tracking certificate returns HOLD.</p><p>The conservative invariant actuator magnitude is at most 9.0635. The proposed bidirectional normalized range is 10. Unbudgeted clipping, slew limits, ringing or crosstalk returns HOLD.</p></div></section>
+<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">04</div><div><h2>Physical component requirements follow the calibrated clock.</h2><p>For physical time scale t₀, sampling period, extra latency and actuator time constant must each be no greater than t₀/400000 seconds. In the example, preload lasts at least t₀/40000 seconds. No numerical hertz is assigned before t₀ calibration; uncertain t₀ requires worst-case normalized bounds.</p><p>Voltage ports require range at least ±10Φ₀/t₀ and current ports at least ±10Q₀/t₀. These are per-channel requirements with the actual canonical scales and loading. The common action S₀ here is distinct from the similarly named structural coefficient in the provenance ledger.</p><p>An ideal 22-bit quantizer leaves room in the proposed input and output error budgets. Nominal bit count alone establishes neither effective resolution nor attainable rate. Additional gain, offset, noise, drift and numerical command errors must fit the remaining allowance.</p></div></section>
+<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">05</div><div><h2>The next gate is apparatus evidence.</h2><p>The machine-readable contract lists actual component and port equations, first-order model identification, range and slew tests, timing records, input/command error decompositions, startup records, finite-calibration regularity and independent coupling/work readout. Every empirical field remains OPEN.</p><p>The result covers a short Gauss-discrimination prefix. It does not certify the full CD-1 campaign or SDW endpoint admission. Non-constructor adversarial review and experimental execution remain pending.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/finite-calibration/">Read the finite-calibration and fault theorem →</a><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/canonical-map/">Read the original canonical map and CD-1 protocol →</a></div></section></main><footer><div><a href="/">Unified Dynamics</a><p>Benjamin Walker Mayes · Independent researcher<br>AI-assisted research · Public atlas</p></div><div><a href="/sources/">Sources & coverage</a><a href="/contribution/">Contribution</a><a href="https://tetrahedral-packet-complexes.ben-w-mayes.chatgpt.site/">Living research portal ↗</a></div><p class="footer-rule">Proof, simulation and physical correspondence retain distinct evidence status. Physical promotion: 0.</p></footer></div></body></html>
